{
  "image_name": "0be1144fa2d5a96254eb310e4219bab2.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways bacterial small RNAs (sRNAs), often with the RNA-binding protein Hfq, control gene expression by acting on mRNAs and RNA stability. Overall, it shows that sRNAs can either repress or activate translation and can also promote or prevent RNA decay.\n\nA main theme is regulation at the ribosome-binding site (RBS). In one case, an mRNA is translated normally when the 30S and 50S ribosomal subunits assemble, but binding of an sRNA–Hfq complex blocks access to the RBS and inhibits translation. In another case, the opposite happens: the mRNA seems to form a structure that hides the RBS, preventing translation, and pairing with an sRNA rearranges the RNA so the 30S subunit can bind and translation proceeds.\n\nThe figure also highlights effects on RNA turnover. Free sRNAs may be degraded by RNase E or stabilized by Hfq. When an sRNA pairs with a target mRNA, the complex can recruit RNase E and trigger degradation of the mRNA. A related pathway appears to involve PAP-mediated polyadenylation followed by exonuclease attack, leading to mRNA breakdown. Together, these mechanisms illustrate the broad post-transcriptional roles of sRNAs.",
